@@ -0,0 +1,479 @@
+{
+ "cells": [
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "# Spherical Deconvolution with Global Constraints"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Constrained Spherical Deconvolution (CSD) *(Tournier et al. 2007)* is one of the most proven ways to estimate Fiber Orientation Distributions (FODs) that describe the orientation of the white matter tissue. In short, CSD's *single-shell* formulation states that any composition of oriented white matter (dispersion/crossings) can be described as the spherical convolution of a *positive* probability density on the sphere and a convolution kernel $K$ that describes one parallel axon bundle. With some abuse of notation:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\\begin{align}\n",
+    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
+    " E_{\\textrm{CSD}}= \\overbrace{\\operatorname{FOD}(\\textbf{c})}^{\\textrm{Fiber Distribution}}\\,*_{\\mathbb{S}^2}\\,\\overbrace{K(\\cdot)}^{\\textrm{Convolution Kernel}}\\quad\\textrm{subject to}\\quad \\operatorname{FOD}(\\textbf{c}) > 0.\n",
+    "\\end{aligned}\n",
+    "\\end{align}$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Here, the FOD is described in terms of a truncated even spherical harmonics series $\\operatorname{FOD}=\\sum_{l=0}^{lmax}\\sum_{m=-l}^l\\textbf{c}_{lm}Y_{lm}$, with $l$ and $m$ describing the order and moment of the spherical harmonic *(Descoteaux et al. 2006)*. Furthermore, $*_{\\mathbb{S}^2}$ describes a spherical convolution on the $\\mathbb{S}^2$ sphere, and $K$ describes the *rotational* harmonics describing a single axon bundle. The kernel $K$ is typically estimated from the data *(Tournier et al. 2007, Tax et al. 2014)*, and CSD is therefore considered a *model-free* approach.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Here we will describe how the `MultiCompartmentSphericalHarmonicsModel` can be used to fit a CSD model using strict and global positivity constraints *(Dela Haije et al., 2020)*. Multi-compartment CSD models will be discussed in a different example."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Multi-Shell Single-Compartment CSD"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We will reproduce the standard CSD formulation, using only a single Zeppelin kernel describing a white matter bundle. Notice that in this model representation we must define the maximum spherical harmonics order at which to truncate the FOD."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 1,
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "We highly recommend installing numba for faster function execution and model fitting.\n",
+      "We highly recommend installing pathos to take advantage of multicore processing.\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "from dmipy.core.modeling_framework import MultiCompartmentSphericalHarmonicsModel\n",
+    "from dmipy.signal_models import gaussian_models\n",
+    "zeppelin = gaussian_models.G2Zeppelin()\n",
+    "csd_mod = MultiCompartmentSphericalHarmonicsModel([zeppelin], sh_order=8)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "### Response Function Estimation"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Before fitting the FOD, we must first fix the parameters of the Zeppelin convolution kernel. First, we load our example slice of HCP data."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 2,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "This data slice originates from Subject 100307 of the Human Connectome Project, WU-Minn Consortium (Principal Investigators: David Van Essen and Kamil Ugurbil; 1U54MH091657) funded by the 16 NIH Institutes and Centers that support the NIH Blueprint for Neuroscience Research; and by the McDonnell Center for Systems Neuroscience at Washington University.\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "from dmipy.data import saved_data\n",
+    "scheme_hcp, data_hcp = saved_data.wu_minn_hcp_coronal_slice()"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 3,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "\n",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 432x288 with 1 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {
+      "needs_background": "light"
+     },
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
+    "import matplotlib.patches as patches\n",
+    "%matplotlib inline\n",
+    "\n",
+    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1)\n",
+    "ax.imshow(data_hcp[:, 0, :, 0].T, origin='lower')\n",
+    "rect = patches.Rectangle((70,70),20,20,linewidth=1,edgecolor='r',facecolor='none')\n",
+    "ax.add_patch(rect)\n",
+    "ax.set_axis_off()\n",
+    "ax.set_title('HCP coronal slice B0 with ROI');"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We gratefully make use of Dipy's automatic response function estimation. We convert Dmipy's acquisition scheme to a Dipy gradient table using `gtab_dmipy2dipy`."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 4,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "text/plain": [
+       "array([0.00130163, 0.00024266, 0.00024266])"
+      ]
+     },
+     "execution_count": 4,
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "execute_result"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "from dmipy.core.acquisition_scheme import gtab_dmipy2dipy\n",
+    "from dipy.reconst.csdeconv import auto_response_ssst\n",
+    "gtab = gtab_dmipy2dipy(scheme_hcp)\n",
+    "response, ratio = auto_response_ssst(gtab, data_hcp, roi_radii=10, fa_thr=0.7)\n",
+    "lambdas = response[0]\n",
+    "lambdas"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Notice that Dipy's response provides the Zeppelin's eigenvalues in mm$^2$/s, so we must first put them in SI units."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 5,
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "lambda_par = lambdas[0] * 1e-6\n",
+    "lambda_perp = lambdas[1] * 1e-6"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We then fix the parameters of the kernel."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 6,
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "csd_mod.set_fixed_parameter('G2Zeppelin_1_lambda_par', lambda_par)\n",
+    "csd_mod.set_fixed_parameter('G2Zeppelin_1_lambda_perp', lambda_perp)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "### Fitting CSD to HCP data and visualizing features"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We are now ready to fit the CSD model as per usual. Using the default `solver='csd'` option will let the algorithm decide which optimizer to use - if volume fractions need to be estimated it will use `'csd_cvxpy'`, and otherwise the basic CSD `'csd_tournier07'` optimizer proposed by *(Tournier et al. 2007)*.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Using the `'csd'` option will also automatically select whether to enforce that the FOD integrates to one over the sphere (i.e. it is a proper distribution). If a non-voxel-varying convolution kernel is given, it will set `unity_constraint=False`, otherwise it will be set to `True`."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 7,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Setup Tournier07 FOD optimizer in 0.01994037628173828 seconds\n",
+      "Fitting of 8181 voxels complete in 17.824796199798584 seconds.\n",
+      "Average of 0.002178804082605865 seconds per voxel.\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "csd_fit = csd_mod.fit(\n",
+    "    acquisition_scheme=scheme_hcp,\n",
+    "    data=data_hcp,\n",
+    "    mask=data_hcp[..., 0]>0,\n",
+    "    solver='csd')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "The `csd_fit` instance now constains the spherical harmonics of the FOD for every fitted voxel. For the used spherical harmonics order of $8$ that means we have estimated a total of 45 coefficients:"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 8,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "text/plain": [
+       "(145, 1, 145, 45)"
+      ]
+     },
+     "execution_count": 8,
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "execute_result"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "csd_fit.fitted_parameters_vector.shape"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We can estimate two metrics that quantify FOD anisotropy:\n",
+    "- The anisotropy index (AI) following *(Jespersen et al. 2007)*, ranging from 1 for completely anistropic, to zero being isotropic.\n",
+    "- The norm of the laplacian of the spherical harmonics of the FOD, explored in e.g. *(Descoteaux et al. 2006)*, which is zero for isotropic FODs and increasingly positive for more anistropic FODs.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "It is relevant to note that AI is dependent on the value of the spherical mean of the FOD, but the Laplacian-norm is not."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 9,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "\n",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 720x360 with 4 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {
+      "needs_background": "light"
+     },
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "ai = csd_fit.anisotropy_index()\n",
+    "lb = csd_fit.norm_of_laplacian_fod()\n",
+    "\n",
+    "fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=[10, 5])\n",
+    "axs = axs.ravel()\n",
+    "\n",
+    "for counter, (name, values) in enumerate(zip(['Anisotropy Index', 'Norm of FOD Laplacian'], [ai, lb])):\n",
+    "    cf = axs[counter].imshow(values.squeeze().T, origin='lower', interpolation='nearest')\n",
+    "    axs[counter].set_title(name)\n",
+    "    axs[counter].set_axis_off()\n",
+    "    fig.colorbar(cf, ax=axs[counter], shrink=0.8)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "As you can see, the AI and laplacian-norm anisotropy metrics have different contrasts: \n",
+    "- AI shows well the constrast between white and grey matter, but not too much between different white matter areas.\n",
+    "- The laplacian-norm emphasizes anisotropic/coherent white matter areas, but does not show as clear the grey/white matter areas."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "The CSD optimizer proposed by *(Tournier et al. 2007)* uses regularization to penalize negative values of the FODs in specific points. This results in sharp FODs, but does not strictly ensure positivity. We can instead use the setting `solver='csd_cvxpy'` to ensure positivity in these specific points without regularization, which  produces a more robust estimation with less sharp peaks, but at a higher computational cost. Finally we can use strict and global positivity constraints *(Dela Haije et al. 2020)* by using the setting `solver='csd_plus'`, which would be preferred when the accuracy of the estimation is more important than compuation time."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 11,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Setup CSD-PLUS FOD optimizer in 0.018807172775268555 seconds\n",
+      "Fitting of 8181 voxels complete in 4698.131550550461 seconds.\n",
+      "Average of 0.574273505751187 seconds per voxel.\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "csd_plus_fit = csd_mod.fit(\n",
+    "    acquisition_scheme=scheme_hcp,\n",
+    "    data=data_hcp,\n",
+    "    mask=data_hcp[..., 0]>0,\n",
+    "    solver='csd_plus')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 12,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "\n",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 720x360 with 4 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {
+      "needs_background": "light"
+     },
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "ai = csd_plus_fit.anisotropy_index()\n",
+    "lb = csd_plus_fit.norm_of_laplacian_fod()\n",
+    "\n",
+    "fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=[10, 5])\n",
+    "axs = axs.ravel()\n",
+    "\n",
+    "for counter, (name, values) in enumerate(zip(['Anisotropy Index', 'Norm of FOD Laplacian'], [ai, lb])):\n",
+    "    cf = axs[counter].imshow(values.squeeze().T, origin='lower', interpolation='nearest')\n",
+    "    axs[counter].set_title(name)\n",
+    "    axs[counter].set_axis_off()\n",
+    "    fig.colorbar(cf, ax=axs[counter], shrink=0.8)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "### Visualizing Fiber Orientation Distributions"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "As before, we can again visualize the FOD estimated using CSD."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 27,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "from dipy.data import get_sphere\n",
+    "from dipy.viz import window, actor\n",
+    "import numpy as np\n",
+    "from matplotlib import pyplot as plt\n",
+    "\n",
+    "sphere = get_sphere(name='symmetric724')\n",
+    "fods = csd_fit.fod(sphere.vertices)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "scene = window.Scene()\n",
+    "ren = actor.odf_slicer(fods[70:90,:, 70:90], sphere=sphere, colormap='plasma', scale=0.5)\n",
+    "ren.display(y=0)\n",
+    "ren.RotateX(-90)\n",
+    "scene.add(ren)\n",
+    "window.snapshot(scene, fname='fods.png', size=(600, 600));"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We can likewise show the FODs based on the CSD+ reconstruction, which are guaranteed to be non-negative."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "fods = csd_plus_fit.fod(sphere.vertices)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "scene = window.Scene()\n",
+    "ren = actor.odf_slicer(fods[70:90,:, 70:90], sphere=sphere, colormap='plasma', scale=0.5)\n",
+    "ren.display(y=0)\n",
+    "ren.RotateX(-90)\n",
+    "scene.add(ren)\n",
+    "window.snapshot(scene, fname='fods+.png', size=(600, 600));"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Notice that in white matter the FODs are nicely shaped and we have a high AI, but in non-white matter they are very noisy and prominent. This is because the response function that we provided does not resemble the data in these areas, and we therefore get a non-sensical result, which is especially detrimental in partial-volumed voxels at the edge of the ventricles."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## References\n",
+    "- Dela Haije, Tom, Evren Özarslan, and Aasa Feragen. \"Enforcing necessary non-negativity constraints for common diffusion MRI models using sum of squares programming.\" NeuroImage 209 (2020): 116405.\n",
+    "- Descoteaux, Maxime, et al. \"Regularized, fast, and robust analytical Q‐ball imaging.\" Magnetic resonance in medicine 58.3 (2007): 497-510.\n",
+    "- Jespersen, Sune N., et al. \"Modeling dendrite density from magnetic resonance diffusion measurements.\" Neuroimage 34.4 (2007): 1473-1486.\n",
+    "- Jeurissen, Ben, et al. \"Multi-tissue constrained spherical deconvolution for improved analysis of multi-shell diffusion MRI data.\" NeuroImage 103 (2014): 411-426.\n",
+    "- Tax, Chantal MW, et al. \"Recursive calibration of the fiber response function for spherical deconvolution of diffusion MRI data.\" Neuroimage 86 (2014): 67-80.\n",
+    "- Tournier, J-Donald, Fernando Calamante, and Alan Connelly. \"Robust determination of the fibre orientation distribution in diffusion MRI: non-negativity constrained super-resolved spherical deconvolution.\" Neuroimage 35.4 (2007): 1459-1472."
+   ]
+  }
